1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,305
The Progressive Party: The
Colonial-Era Party That

2
00:00:03,385 --> 00:00:06,500
Won Singapore's First
Elections, Then Vanished

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,345
When the results of Singapore's
1955 election were announced,

4
00:00:11,425 --> 00:00:15,300
the Progressive Party had
won 4 of the 25 seats.

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:20,775
It had won the two elections
before that, in 1948 and 1951,

6
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:25,750
and it went into 1955 as
the colony's leading party.

7
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:29,989
Its president, C. C. Tan, lost
his own contest, in Cairnhill,

8
00:00:30,069 --> 00:00:34,600
to David Marshall, whose Labour
Front formed the new government.

9
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,708
Ten months later the Progressives voted to

10
00:00:37,788 --> 00:00:41,503
dissolve their party, and
at the 1959 election the

11
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:45,150
party they had merged
into won no seats at all.

12
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:48,639
Their story is as much about
who was allowed to vote,

13
00:00:48,719 --> 00:00:52,625
and how the votes were divided,
as about the party itself.

14
00:00:52,625 --> 00:00:58,058
The Singapore Progressive Party
was formed on the 25th of August

15
00:00:58,138 --> 00:01:03,650
1947 by three lawyers, C. C. Tan,
John Laycock and N. A. Mallal.

16
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:06,965
The Singapore Free Press
reported the next morning

17
00:01:07,045 --> 00:01:10,496
that the new party intended
to put up candidates for

18
00:01:10,576 --> 00:01:14,175
the Legislative Council and
the Municipal Commission.

19
00:01:14,175 --> 00:01:19,564
Tan, a member of the governor's
Advisory Council, was elected president,

20
00:01:19,644 --> 00:01:23,362
with Dr Abdul Samat, Mallal,
Laycock and Claude da

21
00:01:23,442 --> 00:01:28,075
Silva as vice-presidents and A.
P. Rajah as acting secretary.

22
00:01:28,075 --> 00:01:32,219
The party also set up an
electorate sub-committee to help

23
00:01:32,299 --> 00:01:36,375
members of the public who
wanted to register as voters.

24
00:01:36,375 --> 00:01:39,125
Its programme was gradual.

25
00:01:39,125 --> 00:01:43,810
The Progressives were willing to
work with the British administration

26
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:48,575
towards steady constitutional reform,
enlarging the number of elected

27
00:01:48,655 --> 00:01:53,075
members over time and eventually
forming a cabinet of ministers.

28
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:58,660
Their leaders and much of their support
came from English-educated professionals,

29
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:00,548
and the party was backed by

30
00:02:00,628 --> 00:02:04,475
groups such as the Straits
Chinese British Association.

31
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:09,150
The electorate the Progressives
first faced was very small.

32
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:13,911
In the 1948 election, six
of the Legislative Council's

33
00:02:13,991 --> 00:02:18,751
22 seats were elected, voting
was open only to British

34
00:02:18,831 --> 00:02:21,979
subjects who had chosen to register,

35
00:02:22,059 --> 00:02:27,975
and 22,334 people were on the roll
in a city of more than 940,000.

36
00:02:27,975 --> 00:02:30,769
The Progressive Party won three of the

37
00:02:30,849 --> 00:02:33,875
six seats and C. C. Tan topped the poll.

38
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:37,100
The other three went to independents.

39
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:41,168
At the next election, on
the 10th of April 1951,

40
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:45,050
nine of the council's
25 seats were elected.

41
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:49,994
The Straits Times reported
that about 51 per cent

42
00:02:50,074 --> 00:02:55,200
of the 48,155 registered
voters went to the polls.

43
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:58,896
Six Progressives were elected:
C. C. Tan for Tanglin,

44
00:02:58,976 --> 00:03:04,453
John Laycock for Katong, Thio-Chan-Bee
for Balestier, C. J. Paglar for Changi,

45
00:03:04,533 --> 00:03:08,950
N. A. Mallal for City and H. J.
C. Kulasingha for Bukit Timah.

46
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:12,526
The Labour Party won two
seats, one of them Keppel,

47
00:03:12,606 --> 00:03:16,684
where Lim-Yew-Hock defeated
the Progressive vice-president

48
00:03:16,764 --> 00:03:21,057
A. P. Rajah, and Vilasini Menon
won Seletar as an independent

49
00:03:21,137 --> 00:03:24,650
to become the first woman
elected to the council.

50
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:29,475
The Progressives now held most
of the council's elected seats.

51
00:03:29,475 --> 00:03:33,219
The National Library Board
credits the party with

52
00:03:33,299 --> 00:03:38,604
a role in initiating the bill that
set up the Central Provident Fund,

53
00:03:38,684 --> 00:03:41,025
which began operating in 1955.

54
00:03:41,025 --> 00:03:45,550
The party's limits were the limits
of the system it worked within.

55
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:48,915
Most of the council's members
were still officials or

56
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:52,880
appointed by the governor, and
the electorate that had chosen

57
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:56,600
the Progressives was a small
fraction of the population.

58
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,775
The Rendel constitution
changed both of those things.

59
00:04:00,775 --> 00:04:04,889
For the 1955 election, 25
of the 32 seats in a new

60
00:04:04,969 --> 00:04:09,083
Legislative Assembly were
elected, and voters were

61
00:04:09,163 --> 00:04:13,525
registered automatically
instead of having to apply.

62
00:04:13,525 --> 00:04:16,593
The roll grew to about 300,000,

63
00:04:16,673 --> 00:04:20,125
roughly six times the 1951 figure.

64
00:04:20,125 --> 00:04:24,572
Before then, only British
subjects who applied could vote,

65
00:04:24,652 --> 00:04:29,880
and in 1948 Thio-Chan-Bee had warned
that about 100,000 Straits-born

66
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:34,175
Chinese who were entitled
to register had not done so.

67
00:04:34,175 --> 00:04:38,700
Automatic registration put
people like them on the roll.

68
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:42,151
Several new parties formed
to contest the election,

69
00:04:42,231 --> 00:04:47,700
including the Labour Front, the People's
Action Party and the Democratic Party.

70
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:50,180
The Progressive Party's vote did not

71
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:53,175
shrink in number, even as its share fell.

72
00:04:53,175 --> 00:04:57,545
Its 22 candidates won 38,695 votes,

73
00:04:57,625 --> 00:05:01,105
more than its candidates had

74
00:05:01,185 --> 00:05:05,000
won in 1948 and 1951 combined.

75
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,880
The Labour Front,

76
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:10,650
with 42,300 votes, won 10 seats.

77
00:05:10,650 --> 00:05:14,784
The Progressives, with
24.8 per cent of the vote,

78
00:05:14,864 --> 00:05:20,201
won 4. The Democratic Party won
2, the People's Action Party 3,

79
00:05:20,281 --> 00:05:24,673
the alliance of UMNO, the
MCA and the Malay Union 3,

80
00:05:24,753 --> 00:05:28,370
and independents 3. In
Cairnhill, C. C. Tan

81
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:32,750
polled 2,530 votes against
David Marshall's 3,305.

82
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,981
The Straits Times examined
the result the next

83
00:05:36,061 --> 00:05:39,875
morning in an editorial
headed "Looking For A Party."

84
00:05:39,875 --> 00:05:43,508
It noted that on the afternoon
of polling day both the Labour

85
00:05:43,588 --> 00:05:47,099
Front and the People's Action
Party had appeared reconciled

86
00:05:47,179 --> 00:05:50,751
to a Progressive-Democratic
victory, and it pointed out that

87
00:05:50,831 --> 00:05:54,281
the combined vote of the Labour
Front, the People's Action

88
00:05:54,361 --> 00:05:57,872
Party and the Alliance was some
two thousand fewer than the

89
00:05:57,952 --> 00:06:01,829
combined Progressive and Democratic
vote, yet those three had won

90
00:06:01,909 --> 00:06:05,500
sixteen seats against the
Progressives' and Democrats' six.

91
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:09,988
In fifteen of the twenty-one contests
with three or more candidates,

92
00:06:10,068 --> 00:06:13,225
the winner had polled
less than half the votes.

93
00:06:13,225 --> 00:06:16,796
In four of them, Cairnhill,
Katong, Whampoa and Rochore,

94
00:06:16,876 --> 00:06:20,448
the paper judged that the
Progressives and Democrats had

95
00:06:20,528 --> 00:06:24,375
divided votes that would have
won the seat for one of them.

96
00:06:24,375 --> 00:06:27,500
The arithmetic was only part of it.

97
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,218
Most of the newly registered
voters had never been asked to

98
00:06:31,298 --> 00:06:35,273
choose between the parties before,
and the Labour Front and the

99
00:06:35,353 --> 00:06:39,136
People's Action Party campaigned
for faster progress towards

100
00:06:39,216 --> 00:06:43,400
self-government than the Progressives'
gradual programme offered.

101
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:46,449
The National Library
Board's account puts it

102
00:06:46,529 --> 00:06:49,720
as the Progressives being
displaced by parties

103
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:53,000
able to appeal to a
wider spectrum of people.

104
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,402
As the largest party in opposition,

105
00:06:55,482 --> 00:06:59,525
the Progressives said they
would not enter any coalition.

106
00:06:59,525 --> 00:07:02,387
The two parties that had divided

107
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:05,500
the vote in 1955 joined together.

108
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:10,511
On the 5th of February 1956 the
Progressive Party and the Democratic

109
00:07:10,591 --> 00:07:15,154
Party voted themselves out of
existence, and a few hours later

110
00:07:15,234 --> 00:07:20,325
the members chose C. C. Tan to lead
the new Liberal Socialist Party.

111
00:07:20,325 --> 00:07:23,943
Tan-Eng-Joo, the former
Democratic president,

112
00:07:24,023 --> 00:07:29,285
became chairman of its executive
council, and its general council

113
00:07:29,365 --> 00:07:34,050
of 32 had 21 former Progressives
and 11 former Democrats.

114
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:37,825
The electorate changed
again before the next test.

115
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:41,845
For the general election
of the 30th of May 1959,

116
00:07:41,925 --> 00:07:48,705
all 51 seats were elected, the roll had
almost doubled again to more than 580,000,

117
00:07:48,785 --> 00:07:52,550
and voting was compulsory
for the first time.

118
00:07:52,550 --> 00:07:56,163
The Liberal Socialist
Party put up 32 candidates

119
00:07:56,243 --> 00:07:59,475
and received about 8 per cent of the vote.

120
00:07:59,475 --> 00:08:04,350
It won no seats, and twenty of its
candidates lost their deposits.

121
00:08:04,350 --> 00:08:08,536
The People's Action Party
won 43 of the 51 seats and

122
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:13,375
formed the first government
of a self-governing Singapore.

123
00:08:13,375 --> 00:08:17,037
The chart sets the party's
share of the vote against its

124
00:08:17,117 --> 00:08:20,925
share of the elected seats at
each of the four elections.

125
00:08:20,925 --> 00:08:24,035
In 1948 and 1951 its seats ran

126
00:08:24,115 --> 00:08:27,625
level with or ahead of its votes.

127
00:08:27,625 --> 00:08:32,075
From 1955 the seats fell
faster than the votes.

128
00:08:32,075 --> 00:08:36,525
Part of what shaped the Progressives'
story has changed for good.

129
00:08:36,525 --> 00:08:41,025
Voters have been registered
automatically since 1955,

130
00:08:41,105 --> 00:08:44,654
and voting has been compulsory since 1959,

131
00:08:44,734 --> 00:08:49,838
so the kind of small, self-selected
electorate that returned

132
00:08:49,918 --> 00:08:54,325
the Progressives in 1948
and 1951 no longer exists.

133
00:08:54,325 --> 00:08:56,542
Another part is still in place:

134
00:08:56,622 --> 00:09:01,951
single-member constituencies are
decided by whoever polls the most votes,

135
00:09:02,031 --> 00:09:04,550
whether or not that is a majority.

136
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:07,913
Under that rule, as the
Straits Times worked

137
00:09:07,993 --> 00:09:11,905
out in 1955, two parties
drawing on the same voters

138
00:09:11,985 --> 00:09:15,350
can each do well and
still lose to a third.

139
00:09:15,350 --> 00:09:18,132
Where it fits in the bigger story:

140
00:09:18,212 --> 00:09:22,925
Singapore's political history
is usually told from 1959.

141
00:09:22,925 --> 00:09:27,218
The Progressive Party is easy to
leave out because it did not last,

142
00:09:27,298 --> 00:09:31,265
but it was the party that won
Singapore's first two elections,

143
00:09:31,345 --> 00:09:34,333
and its rise and fall
track the widening of the

144
00:09:34,413 --> 00:09:37,923
vote from a few tens of
thousands of registered British

145
00:09:38,003 --> 00:09:42,050
subjects to more than half a
million citizens obliged to vote.
